WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.055 --> 00:00:09.655
OCD is a mental condition in which a person
has unwanted, intrusive, and repetitive thoughts

00:00:09.655 --> 00:00:11.035
that become obsessions.

00:00:11.035 --> 00:00:16.675
These obsessions then make them do things
that relieve them from these thoughts, a behavior

00:00:16.675 --> 00:00:17.995
known as a compulsion.

00:00:17.995 --> 00:00:22.315
This is why it’s called Obsessive-Compulsive
Disorder.

00:00:24.355 --> 00:00:30.895
OCD tends to develop most frequently in children
10–14 years of age with symptoms that range

00:00:30.895 --> 00:00:32.395
across a wide spectrum.

00:00:32.395 --> 00:00:38.455
Some just have repetitive thoughts, others
experience extreme obsessions that make living

00:00:38.455 --> 00:00:39.955
a normal life difficult.

00:00:39.955 --> 00:00:43.015
For many, it comes and goes in waves.

00:00:44.995 --> 00:00:52.195
Symptoms of OCD are often grouped into four
factors: symmetry, forbidden thoughts, cleaning,

00:00:52.195 --> 00:00:53.695
and hoarding.

00:00:53.695 --> 00:00:58.495
Some might experience other symptoms, and
still others a mix of multiple factors.

00:00:59.095 --> 00:01:04.555
Miko, Ann, Jane, and Joe each have their own
version of OCD.

00:01:06.595 --> 00:01:11.275
Miko is obsessed with symmetry and he can
spend hours sorting books.

00:01:11.275 --> 00:01:16.675
And whenever he enters his library, he has
to leave it the exact way that he came in

00:01:16.675 --> 00:01:17.695
— backward.

00:01:19.915 --> 00:01:24.475
Ann began having “forbidden” negative
thoughts after she lost her mom.

00:01:24.475 --> 00:01:30.175
Ever since she’s constantly afraid that
others around her may die - including herself.

00:01:30.175 --> 00:01:33.415
Her forbidden thoughts can strike at any time.

00:01:33.415 --> 00:01:38.035
Her heart starts pounding, and a terrifying
image crosses her mind.

00:01:38.035 --> 00:01:42.835
To make them go away, she starts biting her
nails until they bleed.

00:01:45.175 --> 00:01:50.035
Jane is obsessed with cleanliness and gets
anxious when things appear dirty.

00:01:50.035 --> 00:01:53.335
She washes her hands like 50 times a day.

00:01:53.335 --> 00:01:58.975
And when she’s done, she washes the soap
too, because in her world everything is covered

00:01:58.975 --> 00:02:02.035
in germs — especially the things she touches.

00:02:04.435 --> 00:02:10.675
Joe is constantly thinking about losing things
and to relieve his anxiety he begins hoarding.

00:02:10.675 --> 00:02:15.115
When leaving his house, he checks the lock
on the door again and again.

00:02:15.115 --> 00:02:19.915
Then, 5 minutes later he starts wondering
if he’s closed the windows.

00:02:19.915 --> 00:02:25.555
And so he returns — thinking he was so obsessed
about locking the door, that he might have

00:02:25.555 --> 00:02:26.635
missed the windows.

00:02:27.715 --> 00:02:33.295
So how come that in a group of people, just
some have OCD?

00:02:35.515 --> 00:02:41.515
People with OCD are often born with a genetic
predisposition with about 1 in 4 having an

00:02:41.515 --> 00:02:43.735
immediate family member with the disorder.

00:02:43.735 --> 00:02:49.915
At some point in life, environmental factors,
such as childhood trauma, can initiate the

00:02:49.915 --> 00:02:53.215
onset of OCD or exacerbate its intensity.

00:02:55.435 --> 00:03:01.315
While the neurobiological origins are still
being studied, brain areas regulating thought

00:03:01.315 --> 00:03:04.735
processing may be disrupted in minds with
the disorder.

00:03:06.535 --> 00:03:13.135
As a result, their thoughts can't shift properly,
and instead get stuck, repeated, and lead

00:03:13.135 --> 00:03:14.335
to a loss of control.

00:03:14.335 --> 00:03:19.675
Someone who has to check if they locked the
door over and over again, has the thought

00:03:19.675 --> 00:03:23.635
“Did I lock?” repeating endlessly without
being resolved.

00:03:25.255 --> 00:03:32.095
While obsessions just seem to appear in one’s
head, compulsions are learned responses that

00:03:32.095 --> 00:03:35.815
help reduce the anxiety associated with intrusive
thoughts.

00:03:37.915 --> 00:03:44.515
So if Jane experiences an ever-present fear
of germs, she reduces her anxiety by washing

00:03:44.515 --> 00:03:45.175
her hands.

00:03:45.175 --> 00:03:50.875
Because this ritual temporarily offers relief,
the probability that she will do it again

00:03:50.875 --> 00:03:52.135
is increased.

00:03:54.235 --> 00:04:00.715
Once the OCD Cycle is established, obsessions
lead to anxiety which triggers the compulsion

00:04:00.715 --> 00:04:03.535
that brings the relief — and then it starts
again.

00:04:05.695 --> 00:04:11.995
As a result, compulsive behavior not only
persists but actually becomes excessive — often

00:04:11.995 --> 00:04:16.195
with negative consequences and makes living
a normal life impossible.

00:04:16.195 --> 00:04:24.355
When you begin having OCD you often aren't
aware of it and, as a result, some of the

00:04:24.355 --> 00:04:28.315
terrible thoughts in your brain lead you to
believe that you are a bad person.

00:04:28.315 --> 00:04:34.495
This is why it's important to seek professional
help to understand and eventually "quiet the

00:04:34.495 --> 00:04:34.995
brain”.

00:04:36.955 --> 00:04:42.175
Treatment of OCD includes:
Cognitive behavioral therapy: to overcome

00:04:42.175 --> 00:04:45.295
intrusive thoughts by not indulging in any
compulsions

00:04:45.295 --> 00:04:51.115
Rumination: focused exposure with response
prevention that focuses on obsessions

00:04:51.115 --> 00:04:57.415
Medication
Or nicknaming to relabel, reattribute, refocus,

00:04:57.415 --> 00:04:59.395
and revalue your thoughts

00:05:01.615 --> 00:05:02.635
What about you?

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:05.515
Have you ever experienced obsessive thoughts?

00:05:05.515 --> 00:05:08.995
And if so, what kind of compulsions did you
turn to?

00:05:08.995 --> 00:05:14.635
Please share your insights and strategies
for dealing with your mental monster in the

